PANEL MATH FORMULAS AND SAMPLE CALCULATIONS

Panel Size is always greater than Sample Size, due to response rates and screening disqualifications.

Definitions: Panel Size equals Total Database Invited. Sample Size equals Completed Surveys Needed. Response Rate equals the percentage of invited who respond.

Margin of Error Benchmark: Three hundred eighty-four completed responses gives a statistically reliable plus or minus five percent margin of error for almost any large population.

Panel Mathematics Formula: Required Panel Size equals Desired Sample Size divided by Expected Response Rate, plus a twenty percent Safety Buffer.

Calculation Example, running one study per month: Desired Sample Size is four hundred completed responses. Expected Response Rate is twenty percent. Initial Invites Required equals four hundred divided by zero-point-two-zero, which equals two thousand respondents. Add a twenty percent buffer, meaning four hundred more respondents, for opt-outs or changed emails. The Ideal Standing Panel Size is two thousand four hundred active panelists.

RECOMMENDED PANEL SIZE GUIDELINES — RULES OF THUMB

B2B Niche or SaaS Products: One hundred fifty to five hundred active participants. B2B professionals have high value but low availability, so a tight, highly engaged circle is best.

B2C or Mid-Market Brands: One thousand five hundred to three thousand active participants. This gives enough padding to run multiple consumer studies per month without survey fatigue.

Broad Global or Enterprise Research: Ten thousand-plus active participants. This is necessary to represent entire international market segments across distinct regions.

